<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>Six dials, and what the airframe has taken.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Six dials show GPS and barometer readings, for fun. The Go+ also has a
    g-meter.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="six-pack">Six dials, and <span class="highlight">what is left in the battery</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Ground speed, horizon, altimeter, turn, track, vertical speed: a playful
        way to show the GPS and barometer readings. Above the middle dial, the battery percentage shows how much flying time is left in
        the device. The other five cannot tell you that. The time since
        takeoff is on the radar, for your logbook.
      </p>
      <p>
        These are not flight instruments. The horizon shows the bank angle a
        coordinated turn would need at that speed. Nothing in the device
        measures bank angle. Nothing here is certified. This is not
        an attitude indicator for flying in cloud.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "sixpack.png", alt: "The six-dial page: ground speed, artificial horizon with the battery percentage above it, altimeter, turn coordinator with its ball, track card and vertical speed" %>
      <figcaption>Six dials</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="g-meter">What the airframe <span class="highlight">has taken</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        The fourth page shows the accelerometer readings without interpreting them. The
        marker shows the current load on the wing. The box around it shows
        the highest and lowest loads so far, and the greatest loads to the
        left and right.
      </p>
      <p>
        The peak readings are for this flight. They reset to zero when the flight timer
        starts. Forward and backward loads have their own strip, because braking and a rough
        landing are not a wing load.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "gmeter.png", alt: "The g-meter page: a marker shows normal (up-and-down) and lateral (side-to-side) loads on a plot, with a box around it showing this flight's peaks, a forward-and-backward strip beside it, and three rows of figures in tenths of a g" %>
      <figcaption>G-meter</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

